  <section class="review-quick" id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick answer</h2>
    <p><strong>AICOM (AIMarket)</strong> scores <strong>60/100 (FADING)</strong> on Hlido&rsquo;s independent, hands-on test (reviewed 2026-08-05). FADING (60) — real technical substance (17 mathematically specified oracles, Ed25519-signed receipts, permissive licensing, genuine self-hosting) and notable intellectual honesty in labelling its own beacon cryptography  Pricing: Open source · Usage-based (free entry point documented).</p>
  </section>
  <section class="review-opinion">
    <p class="review-opinion__byline">Hlido Editor &middot; 2026-08-05</p>
    <div class="review-opinion__body"><p>AICOM proposes a full open economy for autonomous agents: a 13-agent factory that takes a brief through spec, code, QA, security and deploy; a federated hub where shipped capabilities and oracles are signed, indexed and invokable; and pay-per-call settlement over micropayment channels, all self-hosted under MIT/Apache-2.0 with an explicit &quot;no hosted black box — your keys, your data, your host&quot; stance. Seventeen oracles are listed with real mathematical substance (verifiable randomness, optimal transport, persistent homology, graph spectra, Gaussian-process uncertainty), each with Ed25519-signed receipts and optional post-quantum ML-DSA, alongside 377 passing tests. The single most credible thing on the page is the smallest: the Chronos × Platon beacon is labelled &quot;research/prototype crypto honesty&quot;, with post-quantum signing off by default. A project that flags its own cryptography as prototype rather than marketing it as quantum-safe is telling you something true, and it is the reason this scores as high as it does. Everything else argues for caution. The scope — factory, marketplace, protocol, oracles, 3D visualisers, Telegram and Discord bots, YouTube release broadcasting, fleet observability — is enormous for what appears to be a very small team, and breadth at that ratio usually means shallow depth somewhere. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, has no adoption evidence, named users, or third-party audit, and &quot;377 tests green&quot; measures self-authored tests, not correctness. Reviewed from the public surface only.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="review-section" id="why"><h2>Why FADING</h2><p>FADING (60) — real technical substance (17 mathematically specified oracles, Ed25519-signed receipts, permissive licensing, genuine self-hosting) and notable intellectual honesty in labelling its own beacon cryptography as research/prototype with post-quantum signing off by default. Held at FADING because the scope is enormous relative to apparent team size, there is no adoption evidence, no third-party audit, and self-authored passing tests are not independent validation of a system that signs and settles value.</p></section>

  
