  <section class="review-section" id="does-well">
    <h2>What it does well</h2>
    <ul class="review-bullets"><li>Labels its own Chronos × Platon beacon as research/prototype cryptography rather than marketing it as production-grade</li><li>Post-quantum ML-DSA available but off by default — a conservative, correct default</li><li>17 oracles with genuine mathematical specificity, each shipping Ed25519-signed receipts</li><li>Fully self-hosted and permissively licensed (MIT/Apache-2.0) — no hosted black box, forkable in every part</li><li>Coherent compounding loop: factory ships a capability, hub indexes it, next run discovers and reuses it</li><li>Verify step is built into the invocation flow — discover, invoke, verify the signed proof, settle</li></ul>
  </section>
  
  <section class="review-section" id="falls-short">
    <h2>What it fails at</h2>
    <ul class="review-bullets"><li>Scope (factory, marketplace, protocol, oracles, 3D visualisers, bots, broadcasting, observability) is enormous relative to apparent team size</li><li>No adoption evidence, named users, or production deployments on the public surface</li><li>&#39;377 tests green&#39; counts self-authored tests — not independent validation of a value-signing system</li><li>No third-party security audit despite cryptographic signing and on-chain settlement being central</li><li>Hosted on GitHub Pages with no institutional footprint or stated continuity commitment</li><li>Heavy mythological branding (Dioscuri, Helios, Metis, Gaia, Skopos, Chronos, Platon) obscures rather than explains what each component does</li></ul>
  </section>
  
  <section class="review-section review-section--red" id="red-flags">
    <h2>Red flags</h2>
    <ul class="review-bullets"><li>[object Object]</li></ul>
  </section>
  
  <section class="review-section review-section--two" id="who-for">
    <div><h2>Best for</h2><ul class="review-bullets"><li>Researchers and builders exploring self-hosted, non-custodial agent-to-agent economies</li><li>Anyone who needs verifiable-math oracles with signed proofs and is able to audit the code themselves</li><li>Teams that require full self-hosting and permissive licensing over managed convenience</li></ul></div>
    <div><h2>Not recommended for</h2><ul class="review-bullets"><li>Production systems settling real value — the project labels key cryptography as prototype</li><li>Buyers needing an independent security audit before trusting signed receipts</li><li>Teams without the capacity to self-host and evaluate a large multi-component stack</li></ul></div>
  </section>
  
